From 73e2270a1367b622d9cfa1bf69d4247c6049eef8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: auditor <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 22:40:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] hygiene: loopback health bind, bounded decision
 journal, pinned dependencies

INVARIANTS PROTECTED: (1) an unauthenticated endpoint that reports
positions and kill-switch state is not exposed on every interface by
default; (2) the state DB does not grow without bound under a permanently
blocking gate; (3) the versions the suite was green against are recorded.

Findings (audit S17, S19; requirements.txt has no lockfile):
  main.py bound HTTPServer to 0.0.0.0. No secrets leak through it, but
  position and breaker state do.
  The decisions journal writes one row per gate block per tick; with one
  symbol at a 60 s loop and a gate that always blocks, ~1,440 rows/day,
  unbounded over months. Nothing reads it to decide, so bounding it costs
  no safety.
  requirements.txt gives ranges; the versions actually tested were not
  recorded anywhere, so a rebuild could resolve a different numpy.

What changed:
  config.py       HEALTHCHECK_HOST (default 127.0.0.1),
                  DECISIONS_RETENTION_DAYS (default 90, 1..3650)
  main.py         bind to HEALTHCHECK_HOST; prune once at startup
  persistence.py  StateStore.prune_decisions(older_than_days) -> count
  constraints.txt requests==2.33.1 numpy==2.4.4 scipy==1.17.1
                  scikit-learn==1.8.0 (the set 4,953 tests passed with)
  Dockerfile      pip install -r requirements.txt -c constraints.txt;
                  promotion_gate.py added to COPY (patch 0002 introduced
                  a lazy import of it and tests/test_dockerfile.py from
                  patch 0001 caught the omission - the test doing its job)
  tests/test_hygiene_patch.py  3 tests

Note for the Docker HEALTHCHECK: it probes 127.0.0.1, so loopback is
sufficient inside the container. Set HEALTHCHECK_HOST=0.0.0.0 only if an
external prober needs it, and put it behind a network policy.
